WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:02.800
They didn't want me
to create a Zedd song.

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:05.233
They wanted me to create
a piece of music that

00:00:05.233 --> 00:00:06.900
matches what this is all about.

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:14.867
[music playing]

00:00:14.867 --> 00:00:17.266
My first thoughts when
the project came to me

00:00:17.266 --> 00:00:23.266
was, finally, and
excited, because I've made

00:00:23.266 --> 00:00:24.867
classical music in my life.

00:00:24.867 --> 00:00:28.533
I've always wanted to be
more in the scoring world.

00:00:28.533 --> 00:00:30.233
And the way it was
presented to me

00:00:30.233 --> 00:00:34.100
is, it's a project
that Darren Aronofsky

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:35.667
is filming for Nat Geo.

00:00:35.667 --> 00:00:39.433
And I'm a big fan, so I
was immediately interested.

00:00:39.433 --> 00:00:42.533
So what I find really
inspirational about One

00:00:42.533 --> 00:00:46.467
Strange Rock is that it's much
more visually stimulating,

00:00:46.467 --> 00:00:49.567
and it keeps you
engaged with the topic,

00:00:49.567 --> 00:00:53.834
and it was my mission to try to
match that in the audio world.

00:00:53.834 --> 00:00:57.834
So in the series, it's a
lot about seeing things

00:00:57.834 --> 00:00:59.367
from a different perspective.

00:00:59.367 --> 00:01:02.834
And even if you think you
know what a certain thing is,

00:01:02.834 --> 00:01:04.467
looking at it from a
different perspective

00:01:04.467 --> 00:01:06.066
you learn a new side of it.

00:01:06.066 --> 00:01:10.633
And that's the thought behind
this chord progression as well.

00:01:10.633 --> 00:01:16.333
The first half is all one
bass note, and these chords,

00:01:16.333 --> 00:01:19.967
this melody, that's
really haunting.

00:01:19.967 --> 00:01:21.400
You think you've
heard it already.

00:01:28.333 --> 00:01:30.500
But it never really opens up.

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:33.233
It's always this question mark
because of this pedal note.

00:01:33.233 --> 00:01:35.767
It doesn't move.

00:01:35.767 --> 00:01:39.333
And then after that big reveal,
you see that exact same melody,

00:01:39.333 --> 00:01:41.700
the same chords, but with
a different bass note,

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:45.633
and it opens up a completely
different emotion.

00:01:45.633 --> 00:01:49.000
The second step was obviously
recording that piano

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.100
and then adding elements to it--

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:53.667
cinematic moments, some
hits that really grab

00:01:53.667 --> 00:01:58.166
your attention, building a huge
climax, and then the orchestra

00:01:58.166 --> 00:01:59.767
is a big piece.

00:01:59.767 --> 00:02:01.800
I asked my friend Brian Tyler
to help orchestrate this piece.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:08.533
Then it goes back up.

00:02:08.533 --> 00:02:11.133
I'm in the process
of the second half

00:02:11.133 --> 00:02:13.500
of the songs, which is
the big climax where

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.400
the whole orchestra comes in.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:19.734
And [music starts] I
really want to find a way

00:02:19.734 --> 00:02:23.333
to add my elements
to it, that sound

00:02:23.333 --> 00:02:29.100
like Zedd, but keep the epicness
of this choir and orchestra.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:31.033
So I'm still halfway done.

00:02:31.033 --> 00:02:34.867
[music playing]

00:02:34.867 --> 00:02:37.533
I hope that when people
hear this piece of music

00:02:37.533 --> 00:02:39.333
that I made for
One Strange Rock,

00:02:39.333 --> 00:02:44.400
they will realize that
I am capable of writing

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:47.367
emotional music outside of
what they think I can do.

00:02:47.367 --> 00:02:50.033
And I just hope that
people give this a chance

00:02:50.033 --> 00:02:52.667
and really listen to it, and
listen to the intricacies

00:02:52.667 --> 00:02:55.533
and details of the
chord progression,

00:02:55.533 --> 00:02:57.467
and how this musical
piece changes

00:02:57.467 --> 00:03:00.600
you when you listen to it.

